questions.scenario:
You are debugging a production issue where a high-throughput payment ingestion service recently migrated to Java 21 with Virtual Threads (`Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()`). Under peak load, throughput plummets, response latencies spike into tens of seconds, and thread dump analysis reveals hundreds of virtual threads stuck in state `WAITING` or `RUNNING`. You inspect the following code snippet:

java
@Service
public class PaymentProcessingService {
    private final Object paymentLock = new Object();
    private final RestClient restClient;

    public PaymentProcessingService(RestClient.Builder restClientBuilder) {
        this.restClient = restClientBuilder.build();
    }

    public PaymentConfirmation process(PaymentRequest request) {
        synchronized (paymentLock) {
            return restClient.post()
                    .uri("/api/v1/settle")
                    .body(request)
                    .retrieve()
                    .body(PaymentConfirmation.class);
        }
    }
}

You are debugging a production issue where an order management service experiences partial data writes and stale reads. An `@Async` Spring service method returns a `CompletableFuture` and delegates database updates across chained async stages as follows:

java
@Service
public class OrderWorkflowService {
    @Autowired private OrderRepository orderRepository;
    @Autowired private AuditLogRepository auditLogRepository;

    @Transactional
    public CompletableFuture<Void> processOrder(Long orderId) {
        Order order = orderRepository.findById(orderId).orElseThrow();
        order.setStatus(OrderStatus.PROCESSING);
        orderRepository.save(order);

        return CompletableFuture.runAsync(() -> {
            AuditLog log = new AuditLog("Order set to PROCESSING: " + orderId);
            auditLogRepository.save(log);
            order.setStatus(OrderStatus.VERIFIED);
            orderRepository.save(order);
        });
    }
}


In production, if `auditLogRepository.save(log)` throws a runtime exception, the first status change to `OrderStatus.PROCESSING` is committed anyway, while the subsequent updates fail, breaking atomicity.

You are debugging a production issue where an API gateway experiences thread exhaustion and complete unresponsiveness under sudden traffic bursts. The service aggregates results from two downstream dependencies using a custom fixed `ThreadPoolExecutor` via `CompletableFuture`:

java
public class AggregationService {
    private final ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(16);

    public AggregatedResponse fetchDetails(String id) {
        CompletableFuture<UserInfo> userFuture = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
            () -> fetchUser(id), executor);
        CompletableFuture<UserPreferences> prefsFuture = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
            () -> fetchPreferences(id), executor);

        return CompletableFuture.allOf(userFuture, prefsFuture)
            .thenApply(v -> new AggregatedResponse(userFuture.join(), prefsFuture.join()))
            .join();
    }
}


When a traffic spike occurs, incoming HTTP requests arrive concurrently. The service locks up completely, with CPU utilization dropping near 0% while all requests time out.
